---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ProviderTile from '../components/ProviderTile.astro';
import SyncDiagram from '../components/SyncDiagram.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<Layout
  title="MyUsage — One menu bar for every AI coding tool, across every Mac"
  description="Native macOS menu bar app for Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, and Antigravity: actionable capacity pace, model costs, token usage, and multi-Mac aggregation."
>

  <!-- HERO ----------------------------------------------------- -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-text">
      <span class="eyebrow">Menu bar · Native macOS · MIT</span>
      <h1>One menu bar for every <span class="accent">AI coding tool</span>, across every Mac.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        MyUsage talks to Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, and Antigravity, and
        turns limits, pace, model costs, and tokens into one readable
        popover — aggregated across every Mac you own through a folder you
        already sync.
      </p>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/zchan0/MyUsage/releases/latest">
          Download <span class="ver">latest</span>
        </a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="https://github.com/zchan0/MyUsage">
          View source ↗
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-meta">
        <span>macOS 14+</span>
        <span>~8 MB binary</span>
        <span>0 dependencies</span>
        <span>278 tests</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-pop">
      <img
        class="native-shot hero-shot"
        src={`${base}/screenshots/overview.png`}
        alt="MyUsage Overview showing four providers ordered by capacity pressure"
        width="348"
        height="466"
        loading="eager"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHY ------------------------------------------------------ -->
  <section class="section">
    <span class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">Why MyUsage</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">The provider UIs each show only what their device knows.</h2>
    <div class="section-prose">
      <p>If you use Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, or Antigravity — and especially if you use them across <strong>more than one Mac</strong> — the official UIs only show what's happening on the device you're sitting at. You hit a weekly limit on Friday afternoon because your laptop has been chewing through tokens all morning while your desktop's "remaining" number lied to you.</p>
      <p>That's the gap MyUsage fills. Each Mac runs the app, talks to the providers it has credentials for, and writes a tiny JSONL snapshot into a folder you already sync. The popover then shows the <em>aggregate</em> across every Mac sharing that folder. No MyUsage backend exists; the sync transport is whatever you already use.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PRODUCT TOUR -------------------------------------------- -->
  <section class="section">
    <span class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">The actual app</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">Overview for the decision. Detail for the evidence.</h2>
    <div class="product-shots">
      <figure class="product-shot">
        <img
          class="native-shot"
          src={`${base}/screenshots/overview.png`}
          alt="Pressure-ordered provider Overview"
          width="348"
          height="466"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          <strong>Pressure-ordered Overview</strong>
          <span>One provider needs attention; healthy providers stay comparable without competing for focus.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="product-shot detail">
        <img
          class="native-shot"
          src={`${base}/screenshots/codex-detail.png`}
          alt="Codex Detail with capacity, reset credits, model costs, and token totals"
          width="348"
          height="670"
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>
          <strong>Evidence-rich Detail</strong>
          <span>Capacity outcome, reset credits, 30-day model costs, and token totals keep their own clear scopes.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FEATURES ------------------------------------------------- -->
  <section class="section">
    <span class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">What you get</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">A small surface that answers the next question.</h2>

    <div class="features">
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card-head">
          <h3>Actionable capacity pace</h3>
          <p>The fill and pace marker become a familiar <code>reserve</code> or <code>deficit</code>. Once enough data exists, MyUsage adds the outcome: <code>Runs out in…</code> or <code>Lasts until reset</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-card-note">early-window fallback · shared Detail / Overview risk signal</p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card-head">
          <h3>Model costs you can inspect</h3>
          <p>Claude and Codex get a 30-day stacked chart and stable model-cost rows. Hover any day to replace the header with its total and the same rows with daily model costs — no remapping, no layout jump.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-card-note">rolling 30 days by default · daily total + model costs on hover</p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card-head">
          <h3>Token usage, kept honest</h3>
          <p>Total / Input / Output / Cache stay in their own 30-day section instead of being squeezed into a chart tooltip. The totals include every ledger account and synced Mac.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-card-note">ledger wire v3 · v1/v2 compatible · all accounts</p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card-head">
          <h3>Multi-device aggregation</h3>
          <p>Each Mac publishes its daily cost, model, and token ledger under <code>&lt;sync-folder&gt;/devices/&lt;id&gt;/</code>. Use iCloud, Syncthing, Dropbox, or NAS; MyUsage has no backend.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-card-note">BYO sync folder · per-device ownership · no credentials synced</p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card-head">
          <h3>Codex reset-credit inventory</h3>
          <p>The compact summary shows the authoritative available count and nearest expiry. Expand it only when you need every reported rate-limit reset credit and expiry.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-card-note">malformed or contradictory responses are never guessed</p>
      </div>

      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card-head">
          <h3>Limit-pressure notifications</h3>
          <p>Native macOS notifications fire when a tracked limit crosses your warn / crit threshold. The state machine is idempotent, so unchanged usage never double-fires.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="feature-card-note">tunable thresholds · resets on window rollover</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PROVIDERS ------------------------------------------------ -->
  <section class="section">
    <span class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">Providers</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">One reading model, four very different data sources.</h2>
    <table class="providers">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Provider</th>
          <th>Data source</th>
          <th>What you see</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="name"><ProviderTile kind="claude" />Claude Code</td>
          <td class="source">OAuth API<br /><code>~/.claude/.credentials.json</code></td>
          <td class="feats">5 h + weekly capacity · reserve/deficit outcome · per-bucket caps · 30-day model costs + tokens · monthly cost</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="name"><ProviderTile kind="codex" />Codex</td>
          <td class="source">OAuth API<br /><code>~/.codex/auth.json</code></td>
          <td class="feats">5 h + weekly capacity · reserve/deficit outcome · reset credits · 30-day model costs + tokens · monthly cost</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="name"><ProviderTile kind="cursor" />Cursor</td>
          <td class="source">Local SQLite + Connect RPC<br /><code>state.vscdb</code></td>
          <td class="feats">Included quota + on-demand budget bars · billing-cycle countdown</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="name"><ProviderTile kind="antigravity" />Antigravity</td>
          <td class="source">Local language server probe</td>
          <td class="feats">Per-model quota bars · IDE running indicator</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <!-- ARCHITECTURE -------------------------------------------- -->
  <section class="section">
    <span class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">Architecture</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">A folder you already sync.</h2>
    <div class="section-prose">
      <p>The "no backend" claim is load-bearing — there is genuinely no MyUsage-operated server anywhere. Each Mac writes a per-device snapshot into a folder you already sync (iCloud, Syncthing, Dropbox, NFS, anything). The popover reads every other device's folder and totals them.</p>
    </div>
    <SyncDiagram />
    <div class="section-prose">
      <p>The device ID is a salted SHA-256 of <code>IOPlatformUUID</code>, reshaped to RFC 4122 UUIDv4 form, cached in <code>UserDefaults</code>. Reinstalling MyUsage on a Mac re-derives the same ID. Read the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/docs/multi-device-sync`}>multi-device sync deep dive</a> for the full design and edge cases.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- INSTALL SUMMARY ----------------------------------------- -->
  <section class="section">
    <span class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">Install</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">Three drags, then it's running.</h2>
    <div class="section-prose">
      <ol>
        <li>Download the latest <code>MyUsage-&lt;version&gt;.zip</code> from <a href="https://github.com/zchan0/MyUsage/releases/latest">GitHub Releases</a>, unzip.</li>
        <li>Drag <code>MyUsage.app</code> to <code>/Applications/</code>.</li>
        <li>First launch: right-click → <strong>Open</strong> (Gatekeeper will prompt once, then remember).</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="callout">
        <div>
          <strong>Why the Gatekeeper warning?</strong> MyUsage is ad-hoc signed (no paid Apple Developer certificate). The binary is the same one published in GitHub Releases — verify with the attached <code>.sha256</code>. See the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/install`}>full install guide</a> if Gatekeeper blocks you.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BACKGROUND ---------------------------------------------- -->
  <section class="section">
    <span class="eyebrow section-eyebrow">Project background</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">Built for this exact problem, by someone who has it.</h2>
    <div class="section-prose">
      <p>MyUsage is open source under MIT, written entirely in Swift 6 / SwiftUI. The codebase ships 278 tests covering parsing, pace calculations, sync, cost attribution, credentials, and notifications. The whole binary is under 8 MB because there are zero third-party dependencies — everything runs on first-party Apple frameworks.</p>
      <p>The development story (multi-device sync architecture, locally-computed cost pipeline) lives in the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/blog`}>blog</a>. Release history in the <a href="https://github.com/zchan0/MyUsage/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md">CHANGELOG</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</Layout>
